{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB11978 - MONDO_17893",
  "verbalization": "Glasdegib is associated with Inherited Acute Myeloid Leukemia through a chain of indications involving Mitoxantrone. The drug is linked to Acute Myeloid Leukemia with specific genetic mutations, such as Npm1 somatic mutations, Inv3(p21;q26.2), or T(3;3)(p21;q26.2), all of which are known to be associated with more aggressive forms of the disease. Mitoxantrone, a chemotherapy agent, is indicated for these subtypes of Acute Myeloid Leukemia and is further linked to Inherited Acute Myeloid Leukemia. This suggests a potential pathway where Glasdegib may influence the progression or treatment response in patients with these genetic profiles, possibly through mechanisms that affect the efficacy of Mitoxantrone or by targeting pathways involved in leukemogenesis. The evidence supports a role for Glasdegib in the management of this condition, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
